;; Calling a bjoroutine from an ordinary function.
;;
;; The direct style means the call site of a bjoroutine looks like any other
;; call — there is no `await` to forget. The price of that is that the *caller*
;; has to be a bjoroutine too, because the C# it compiles to is an `await` and
;; an `await` needs an async method around it.
;;
;; Caught here rather than by Roslyn: the failure otherwise lands in generated
;; code, in a method the user never wrote, naming a type (`Fiber<int>`) that
;; does not exist in the language.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: calling 'fetch' is a yield point
;; EXPECT-ERROR: yield point is not allowed here
;; EXPECT-ERROR: 'use-it' is defined with (defun ...)
;; EXPECT-ERROR: Define it with (defbjo ...)

(: fetch (-> int int))
(defbjo (fetch x) (* x 2))

(: use-it (-> int int))
(defun (use-it x) (fetch x))

(defun (main args) 0)
